Time Series Analysis

autoregressive moving average model (ARMA) used in predicting marathon runners' results (2020)
  used several years of data to predict upcoming competition results
  https://sciendo.com/pdf/10.2478/ijcss-2022-0008#:~:text=Time%20series%20analysis%20in%20sports,marathon%20results%20over%20several%20years

player styles could significantly skew data
  for example, a team playing a 4-2-1-2-1 (very defensive) would have different defender expectations than one in a 3-4-3
  does this have a significant effect on data?
  https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10443261/

not pursuing time series analysis:
  data is not organized enough to be easy to split
  team bias is too strong (good player w/ bad team and vice versa)
  players switch teams too often
  too many new players (regression to the mean, https://www.iwh.on.ca/what-researchers-mean-by/regression-to-mean)

possible angle for working against team bias:
  compare all stats except win percentage, then negatively bias
    e.g., player A has stats near 50th percentile of defenders, but team wins 20% of matches. that player is probably much better than 50th percentile.
    similar to explanation for elo (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S036013151630080X), we want to reward players with good performance in tough situations
